  "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
